{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "The evidence mapping records a Lean proof of exactly this statement. Lean status per paper is kept in authored/evidence-status.json.", "citation_ids": ["cite.long.thm-critical-dyadic-band"], "evidence_ids": ["mapping.long.thm-critical-dyadic-band"], "id": "long.thm-critical-dyadic-band", "kind": "claim", "label": "Long record Theorem 6.43 (Reduction to the nearest dyadic boundary)", "locator": "Long record Theorem 6.43, p. 56, label thm:critical-dyadic-band", "source_id": "paper.long.pdf", "statement": "*Let $d,E$ be nonnegative integers with $E\le2^{d+1}$, and let $j_*$ be the largest $j\in\{0,\ldots,d\}$ for which $E\le2^{d-j+1}$. The set is nonempty, so $j_*$ is well defined, including when $E=0$. Then
$$
\begin{aligned}
 &\forall j\in\{0,\ldots,d\},\quad
  2^{d-j+1}<E\ \text{or}\ E+2(d+j)\le2^{d-j+1}\\
 &\hspace{35mm}\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 E+2(d+j_*)\le2^{d-j_*+1}.
 \end{aligned}
